<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thurgau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Canton of Thurgau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thurgau is a region and canton of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenfeld">Frauenfeld</a>. Once a large Alemannic district, it was held largely by the counts of Kyburg and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> until the Swiss Confederates conquered it in 1460. Ruled as a common lordship of the cantons, it became a full canton in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thurgau.jpg" alt="Thurgau" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tschubby · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Kanton_Thurgau_2010.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thurgau is one of the 26 cantons of the Swiss Confederation, in Eastern Switzerland, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenfeld">Frauenfeld</a>. It is named after the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a> and is bounded to the north by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, with the High Rhine forming part of the border in the northwest. The name Thurgovia was historically used for a larger area that took in much of the modern canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>, the Appenzell lands and the eastern parts of the canton of Zurich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period the land was inhabited by the Pfyn culture, named after Pfyn on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. In Roman times it belonged to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> until 450, when the Alemanni settled; Roman settlements included Ad Fines (Pfyn), Arbor Felix and Tasgetium. In the sixth century Thurgovia became a Gau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> as part of Alamannia, and it passed to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> in the early 10th century. Its most important early medieval cities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>, seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>, home of the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a>.</p><p>The dukes of Zähringen and the counts of Kyburg took over much of the land in the High Middle Ages. Zurich left Thurgau in 1218, and when the Kyburg dynasty became extinct in 1264 the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> took over its lands. In 1460 the Old Swiss Confederacy, allied with ten freed bailiwicks of the former Toggenburg, seized the territory from the Habsburgs, and it became a subject territory of seven cantons: Zurich, Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz, Unterwalden, Zug and Glarus.</p><p>In 1524 local peasants occupied the cloister of Ittingen, driving out the monks. Between 1526 and 1531 most of the population adopted the Reformed faith from Zurich, but Zurich's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-kappel">Second War of Kappel</a> in 1531 ended Reformed predominance, and the First Peace of Kappel protected both faiths. Zurich briefly occupied the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-villmergen">First War of Villmergen</a> in 1656. Thurgau became a canton in 1798 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> and a member of the Confederation in 1803 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The canton has five districts and 282,909 inhabitants in December 2020, and is mostly German speaking. Its current constitution dates from 1987. Thurgau is known for apples and pears, many of them grown for cider, and for wine from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a> valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thurgau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within the Confederacy Zürich held the leading position as the Vorort, convening the federal Diet and conducting common business, and it shared with other cantons the government of common lordships such as Baden and the <b>Thurgau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The cantons jointly ruled conquered territories such as the Aargau, taken from the Habsburgs in 1415, <b>Thurgau</b>, taken in 1460, and the Italian-speaking bailiwicks south of the Alps, sending bailiffs in rotation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It restored a confederation of largely self-governing cantons, now nineteen: the thirteen members of the Old Swiss Confederacy and six new cantons formed from former subject and allied lands, namely St. Gallen, Graubünden, Aargau, <b>Thurgau</b>, Ticino and Vaud.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts combined allodial land, advocacies over monasteries and comital rights in the Aargau, <b>Thurgau</b> and Zürichgau.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li></ul></section>
